## Claim
Text: "Wearing a face mask can cause pleurisy."
Claim date: September 07, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### 1. What are the established medical causes and common risk factors for developing pleurisy?
Answer: According to the source, the established medical causes of pleurisy include infection of the lower respiratory system by a virus or bacteria (particularly in young, healthy people), pneumothorax (air leaking into the pleural cavity), chest injuries such as broken ribs, tuberculosis or other infections, tumours in the pleura, autoimmune diseases such as rheumatoid arthritis and lupus, sickle cell crisis, pulmonary embolism, pancreatitis, and as a complication of heart surgery. The source does not list separate "risk factors" per se, but it notes that "many different health problems can lead to pleurisy," and it makes no mention of face masks as a cause.

Source URL: https://myhealth.alberta.ca/Health/Pages/conditions.aspx?hwid=tj8148&lang=en-ca

### 2. Is there any published medical or scientific literature that presents evidence of face masks directly causing or contributing to the development of pleurisy?
Answer: No, the search result indicates that health experts dismiss the claim and state there is no evidence that face masks cause or contribute to pleurisy; for instance, the president of the Dutch Association of Doctors for Lung Diseases called the claim "complete nonsense," and the Australian Department of Health does not list face masks as a cause of the condition.

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/wearing-face-mask-does-not-put-you-risk-developing-pleurisy-health-experts-say

### 3. Have any health authorities (e.g., WHO, CDC, national medical associations) stated that wearing face masks can cause pleurisy?
Answer: No, the search result indicates that health authorities and experts have explicitly dismissed this claim. According to the AFP Fact Check article, "pulmonologists say wearing a face mask does not put you at risk of developing pleurisy," and the Australian state of Victoria's Department of Health does not list masks as a cause for the condition, attributing it instead to infections, cancer, or autoimmune conditions.

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/wearing-face-mask-does-not-put-you-risk-developing-pleurisy-health-experts-say

### 4. Is there a plausible biological or physiological mechanism by which wearing a face mask could cause inflammation of the pleural membranes?
Answer: According to the source, health experts have dismissed the claim, stating there is no plausible biological mechanism because a mask does not significantly alter oxygen intake, carbon dioxide levels, or bacterial exposure in a way that could cause pleurisy. As one pulmonologist explained, "Air passes around and through the face mask so there’s no question about it causing a lack of oxygen," and pleurisy is known to be caused by infections, clots, or autoimmune conditions—not by wearing a mask.

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/wearing-face-mask-does-not-put-you-risk-developing-pleurisy-health-experts-say

### 5. What do pulmonology experts say about the potential for face masks to affect the pleura or the respiratory system in a way that leads to inflammation?
Answer: Pulmonology experts state that wearing a face mask does not cause pleurisy or lead to inflammation of the pleura or respiratory system. Dr. Leon van den Toorn, president of the Dutch Association of Doctors for Lung Diseases and Tuberculosis, dismissed the claim as “complete nonsense,” adding, “There is simply no indication that a face mask carries any health risk whatsoever.” Dr. Hans in ‘t Veen, a pulmonologist, also confirmed, “A face mask used normally will not give you an infection in the lungs.”

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/wearing-face-mask-does-not-put-you-risk-developing-pleurisy-health-experts-say

