Answer: No documented case reports exist where pleurisy was clinically attributed to face mask use; health experts have dismissed such claims as "complete nonsense," stating there is "simply no indication that a face mask carries any health risk whatsoever."

Source URL: https://factcheck.afp.com/wearing-face-mask-does-not-put-you-risk-developing-pleurisy-health-experts-say

### 7. Could the symptoms attributed to "mask-caused pleurisy" be explained by other conditions more commonly associated with mask use, such as costochondritis, anxiety-related chest pain, or respiratory muscle strain?
Answer: Yes, symptoms attributed to "mask-caused pleurisy" could plausibly be explained by conditions like costochondritis or anxiety-related chest pain, which are more commonly associated with mask use. As the source describes, costochondritis causes sharp, localized chest pain that is "worse with movement, exertion and deep breathing" and can be triggered by "repeated bouts of coughing"—potential effects of prolonged mask wear. Furthermore, the source explicitly notes that "anxiety is a common cause of chest pain," which could also be exacerbated by mask discomfort or stress.

Source URL: https://patient.info/signs-symptoms/chest-pain-leaflet/costochondritis

